---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { forum, site } from '../data/site.js';

const inquire = `mailto:${site.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('The Lion Forum')}`;

/* The claim turns on two verbs. Setting just those two in the serif italic
   (.em, a base.css primitive) gives one display line two voices, which is
   the difference between a sentence that is large and a sentence that is
   composed. The copy itself stays one plain string in src/data/site.js. */
const claim = forum.line
  .replace(/\bbuild\b/, '<em>build</em>')
  .replace(/\bdecide\b/, '<em>decide</em>');
---
{/*
  FIVE BEATS, AND NO TWO OF THEM ARE DRAWN THE SAME WAY.

    1  masthead   the photograph wide, and the ONLY place on this site where
                   the picture, not the grid, decides where type goes: the
                   name is set into the frame's empty half
    2  the claim   type only, flush left, nothing beside it
    3  the ledger  the reading size, and the page's first change of axis:
                   three terms hanging right-aligned against three sentences
    4  the plate   the one picture on the site with an edge on all four
                   sides: a bounded square hung at the rail, cut below the
                   waterline, carrying no type at all
    5  the close   THE PAGE TURNED OVER

  THE FRAME HAD AN EMPTY HALF AND THE PAGE WAS NOT USING IT. The sloop, its
  two masts and the breakwater all live in the left 62% of the masthead; the
  right 540px was open water and sky carrying nothing, while the display stack
  sat at x=51 with the forward mast passing through the "T" of "The". That is
  a collision, not an interlock. The name now hangs off the RIGHT rail in the
  water, two lines deep, and the subject keeps its own third; the scrim is
  MIRRORED to match (align="right"), so the ink is laid where the words are
  and the boat keeps its light. The kicker and the dateline came out of the
  stack — at 55–60% of the frame they sat on L* 62 sky, four points past what
  the micro register can carry — and are now the left end of the foot band,
  where the ramp is at full weight. Small and dark at the lower left, enormous
  and light at the upper right, one rule running between them.

  THE EXCEPTION, AND THERE IS ONLY ONE ON THE PAGE GROUND. Everything between
  §2 and §4 is cream type on navy opening flush at x=51. §5 is full-bleed
  cream arriving directly off the navy ground: L* 15 → L* 92, the largest tonal step the system owns, spent
  once, on the only thing a foundation officer came to this page to find out —
  what the Institute's relationship to this convening actually is. It is also
  the one beat that does not begin at the rail: the eyebrow holds a narrow
  left rail, the sentence starts at ~34% at --step-feature (the series-breaker,
  used ONCE on this page, here) and runs into the right-hand column the rest
  of the page leaves empty. Restraint plus one exception is composition.

  GROUND BUDGET: hero photograph · cream. The plate no longer spends a ground
  event — bounded, it is an object on the navy rather than a surface. The
  ledger's .on-panel band came off for the same accounting: a 13-point step on
  a 255 scale was spending a ground event and reading as nothing.

  THE PLATE IS NOT THE MASTHEAD AGAIN. "One photograph, seen twice — wide,
  then close" was the right idea and the wrong execution twice over: first by
  browser zoom, then by a cut file that was still a ~1.75x centre crop of the
  same rectangle run FULL-BLEED, which kept the sky, the horizon, the
  breakwater, the tower, the runabout and the sloop in the same arrangement.
  The master cannot yield a genuine close at full bleed (hull + reflection is
  ~520x340 native), so beat 4 stopped being full-bleed. It is a 32rem square
  hung at the shell's left rail, cut at x 655 / y 832 of the master, 405
  square: transom, mast reflection, two buoys, water. Nothing above the
  waterline. A different KIND of frame carrying a different subject, which is
  the premise of the page. It carries no type — the bridge line that used to
  sit on the water was a fourth slogan on a page already being sold with
  slogans. Its one mark is the photograph's own credit, in Figure's corner
  voice, in the deep zone of a bottom-left ramp.

  THE ASK IS SINGULAR. It is made once, at the close. The footer's secondary
  door points at this page too, and Footer.astro drops it on this route by its
  own test — this file used to force that from outside with !important, which
  is a shared component rule rewritten from a route, and it is gone.

  TWO LOCATION STRINGS, TWO JOBS, and they no longer share a viewport.
  "Kennedy Compound, Hyannis Port" describes the CONVENING and stays in the
  masthead's display stack at the bridge rung. "Block Island, Rhode Island"
  describes the PHOTOGRAPH and has moved to the plate, 2000px down the page,
  in the dim credit register. See refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md.

  Motion ranks the page by KIND, not only by delay: the masthead lifts its
  lines on load, the claim SETTLES under scroll and its two italic verbs
  arrive behind the roman so the hinge of the sentence is its own event, the
  plate WIPES open on both axes and then APPROACHES (1.055 → 1.000,
  scroll-linked), the ledger and the close reveal.

  Ladder, top to bottom: 126 banner · 86 poster · 67 feature · 26.6 bridge ·
  19 lede · 16 body · 13.1 index · 11.7 micro.
*/}<Base title="Forum" description="The Lion Forum: an invitation-only convening at the Kennedy Compound, Hyannis Port.">
  <!-- 1 ── the masthead. Kicker, dateline, name: the ladder the hero was
       missing, so 11.7px never stands directly against 126px again.

       TWO LOCATION STRINGS, TWO JOBS, and they are not allowed to collapse
       into one. "Kennedy Compound, Hyannis Port" describes the CONVENING, so
       it sits inside the display stack, in the bridge voice, one line under
       the name. "Block Island, Rhode Island" describes the PHOTOGRAPH, so it
       sits in the opposite corner in the dim credit register that every
       full-bleed scene on this site uses. See refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md. -->
  <section class="fh">
    {/* The credit is not optional on this frame, it is the frame's whole
        problem. The event line below reads as a caption the moment it is the
        only place-name on the picture, and the picture is a harbour on Block
        Island, 200 miles from Hyannis Port. Credit top-right, opposite the
        event line in the foot band: two location strings, two corners, two
        registers, so a reader cannot collapse them. refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md. */}
    {/* NOT forum.image. The shared @1200 sibling of the wide frame is
        1200x536, and this box on a phone is 398x1016 CSS — 796x2032 device
        pixels at DPR2, a 3.79x vertical upscale of a landscape file. This
        route carries its OWN pair: forum-mast.jpg is the same wide frame for
        the desktop box, and forum-mast@1200.jpg is a PORTRAIT cut at native
        resolution (490x1250, the tallest the source holds) so the phone is
        served a picture shaped like the hole it goes in. */}
    <Figure src="/media/forum-mast.jpg" alt="" ratio="auto" grade={0.6}
            focal="40% 50%" focalSm="50% 50%"
            parallax={6} scrim="bottom" align="right" reach={0.60} rise={0.62}
            loading="eager"
            credit={forum.credit} creditPos="top-right"
            class="fh__bg" />

    <div class="shell fh__in">
      <div class="fh__lock">
        <h1 class="display display--poster fh__h lines">{forum.name}</h1>
      </div>

      <div class="fh__bar">
        <div class="fh__meta">
          <p class="eyebrow fh__brow">{forum.access}</p>
          <p class="bridge fh__place">{forum.place}</p>
        </div>
        <span class="fh__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <!-- Points at the SECTION, not its heading. The h2 carries
             data-settle, so it is transformed while it arrives and the
             browser scrolls to a position it is no longer at — the claim
             landed 33px above the viewport top, under the fixed bar. The
             section is untransformed and is what "Scroll" means anyway. -->
        <a class="cue tap fh__cue" href="#fsay">
          <span class="label">Scroll</span><i class="cue__line" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 ── the claim. Flush left, nothing in a margin beside it, and the
       only thing on the page that moves with the scroll rather than at it. -->
  <section class="fsay" id="fsay" aria-labelledby="fsay-h">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="frow">
        <span class="frow__rule" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="left"></span>
        <span class="index fnum" data-reveal="220">01</span>
      </div>

      <div class="fsay__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The convening</p>
      </div>

      <div class="fsay__claim" data-reveal>
        <h2 class="display display--poster fsay__p" id="fsay-h" data-settle="0.52" set:html={claim}></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 ── THE LEDGER, and the page's one change of axis.

       This page sold the Forum with four slogans and a mailto. A programme
       officer who wanted to know what the convening actually IS had nothing
       on it to read: five sizes in main and not one of them a reading size.
       This beat is the reading size, and it is the only thing on the page
       that does not begin at the left rail.

       Three entries, one sentence each — the brief's three-supporting-line
       limit spent, once, on the page's actual job, and nothing else in this
       beat is allowed to spend it. The terms hang right-aligned in
       the left column against the sentences, so the block reads as a ledger
       and its axis sits near 38% of the measure. Every other beat here opens
       flush at the rail; this one does not, which is the whole point.

       It sits on the page ground. It used to be an .on-panel band, but a
       13-point step on a 255 scale is not a tonal event and it was costing
       one of the page's three ground changes; the rules do the ranking and
       the change of value is spent on §5 instead. The hairlines are pulled
       back to the text measure — they used to over-span the sentences under
       them by 419px in all four rows. -->
  <section class="fbrief" aria-labelledby="fbrief-h">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="frow">
        <span class="frow__rule" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="left"></span>
        <span class="index fnum" data-reveal="220">02</span>
      </div>

      {/* The lede came out. refs/BRIEF.md caps a section at three short
          lines of supporting text; the three rows below ARE those three, and
          the lede was a fourth string set as a sentence in .lede — two more
          lines at 1440, four on a phone. The eyebrow names the beat. */}
      <div class="fdef fbrief__top" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow fdef__t" id="fbrief-h">{forum.brief.eyebrow}</p>
      </div>

      <dl class="fdefs" data-seq="120" data-seq-from="40">
        {forum.brief.rows.map((r) => (
          <div class="fdef fdef--row" data-reveal>
            <dt class="label fdef__t">{r.term}</dt>
            <dd class="fdef__d">{r.text}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 ── THE PLATE, AND IT IS NOT A SECOND PHOTOGRAPH OF THE SAME THING.

       It was. The plate was a ~1.75x centre crop of the masthead run
       full-bleed, which meant it still carried the sky, the cloud bank, the
       horizon, the full-width breakwater, the lattice tower, the runabout,
       the sloop, the buoy and the pilings — the same objects in the same
       arrangement, at nearly the same sky-to-water ratio. A reader scrolling
       past y≈2050 met the y=0 photograph a second time; on a phone the
       portrait sibling centred the same sloop 1300px below the masthead.

       The 2800x1250 master cannot yield a genuine close at full bleed — the
       hull and its reflection are ~520x340 native, a 2.8x upscale across
       1440 — so the fix is structural rather than another crop of the same
       rectangle: THE PLATE IS NO LONGER FULL-BLEED. It is a bounded square
       hung at the shell's left rail, 405px native rendered into a 32rem box,
       and the crop is taken below the waterline (x 655, y 832 of the master,
       405 square, the top 4% of it eaten by Figure's overscan): the transom, the mast's reflection running the full height
       of the frame, two buoys, and water. No sky, no cloud, no horizon, no
       breakwater, no tower, no second boat. Beat 4 is now a different KIND of
       frame carrying a different subject — a plate, not a screen — which is
       what "five beats, and no two of them are drawn the same way" asked for.

       It carries no type; its one mark is the photograph's own credit. It
       opens on both axes, then closes the last five percent of its scale as
       you scroll it. -->
  <div class="fplate">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="fplate__frame" data-wipe="center-y">
        <div class="fplate__in" data-settle="0.42">
          <Figure src="/media/forum-plate.jpg"
                  alt="A moored hull below the waterline: the transom, the mast's reflection, and two buoys on still water."
                  ratio="auto" grade={0.44} grain={0.85}
                  focal="50% 50%" focalSm="50% 50%"
                  scrim="none" vig={0.14} lift={1.08}
                  credit={forum.credit} creditPos="bottom-left"
                  parallax={0} sizes="(max-width: 860px) 100vw, 32rem" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5 ── THE CLOSE, and the page's one exception.

       Everything above this line is cream type on navy, opening flush at the
       left rail. This is the page turned over: full-bleed cream, arriving
       directly off the dark plate, so the largest tonal step available on the
       site — L* 15 to L* 92 — lands on the one thing a programme officer came
       here to find out, which is what the Institute's relationship to this
       convening actually is.

       It is also the one beat that does not begin at x=51. The rule and the
       numeral quote the page's own row construction so the break is a return
       and not a stray; below it the composition shifts right, the sentence is
       set at --step-feature (the series-breaker, used ONCE, here), and it runs
       into the 460px of right-hand column the rest of the page left empty.

       The footer's quieter "Inquire about the Lion Forum" door points at this
       page, so Footer.astro does not render it on this route. That test lives
       in the footer; this file no longer reaches in to hide it. -->
  <section class="on-cream frole" aria-labelledby="frole-h" data-settle="0.86">
    <span class="frole__cut" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <div class="shell frole__shell">
      <div class="frow">
        <span class="frow__rule" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="left"></span>
        <span class="index fnum" data-reveal="220">03</span>
      </div>

      <div class="frole__grid">
        <p class="eyebrow frole__brow" data-reveal>The Institute&rsquo;s role</p>

        <div class="frole__body" data-seq="150" data-seq-from="60">
          <h2 class="display display--feature frole__p" id="frole-h" data-reveal>{forum.role}</h2>
          <p class="frole__note" data-reveal="120">{forum.roleNote}</p>

          <a class="frole__cta" href={inquire} data-reveal="240">
            <span class="frole__ctaRule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="bridge frole__ctaText">
              {forum.cta}
              <svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 22 10" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M16 1l5 4-5 4M21 5H0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" fill="none"/></svg>
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  /* THE BAR IS NOT THIS PAGE'S TO EDIT. This file used to reach into
     :global(.nav.is-scrolled)::before and replace both the Nav's --nav-tail
     (3.5rem, deliberately reduced there) and its two-stop --nav-a/--nav-b/
     --nav-cut gradient with a single ramp. That is a shared component rule
     rewritten from a route file, and it is gone. If the hero display needs a
     deeper scrim under the bar, it is a change to Nav.astro, reported — not
     taken here. The same went for the footer's secondary door: this route
     used to hide .foot__second with the piece's only !important. That
     question belongs to Footer.astro, which now answers it with its own
     isHere('/forum/') test — the door is not rendered rather than rendered
     and painted out. */

  /* ── 1. The masthead ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .fh {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100svh;
    display: grid;
    isolation: isolate;
  }
  .fh__bg { position: absolute; inset: 0; aspect-ratio: auto; z-index: -1; }
  .fh__in {
    width: 100%;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
    align-content: end;
    padding-block: var(--nav-h) clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2.6rem);
  }
  /* TYPE SET INTO THE NEGATIVE SPACE, not at the rail.

     This photograph has an empty half. The sloop, its two masts and the
     breakwater all live in the left 62%; the right 540px is open water,
     horizon and sky carrying nothing. The display stack used to sit at
     x=51, which put 126px serif directly across the rigging — the forward
     mast passed through the "T" of "The". A collision, not an interlock.

     The stack now hangs off the RIGHT rail, in the water, and the picture's
     subject is left alone in its own third. It is the only beat on the page
     that does not open at x=51, and the only place on the site where a
     photograph's composition, rather than the grid, decides where type goes.
     The scrim is mirrored to match (align="right" on the Figure) so the ink
     is laid where the words actually are and the sloop keeps its light. */
  .fh__lock {
    align-self: end;
    justify-self: end;
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: min(100%, 42ch);
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* THE BRIDGE. The event's location, in the display stack with the name and
     in the page's mid voice — never in the credit register, which belongs to
     the photograph and says Block Island. */
  /* 9ch turns the name over after "Lion", so the name is a two-line block
     ~470px wide that sits entirely in the open water at the right of the
     frame. The picture's subject keeps its own third and the page's largest
     type stands in the empty one — neither is in front of the other. */
  .fh__h { max-width: 9ch; }

  /* The bar left the lock: it now spans the full shell, so the rule runs the
     whole horizon from the empty left rail to the cue, and the display block
     hanging above its right end is measured by it. */
  /* THE FOOT BAND. The kicker and the dateline used to sit above the name,
     which put 12px micro-caps and a 27px bridge line at 55–60% of the frame
     — over open sky at L* 57–62, four points past what the register can
     carry, and the meter said so. They belong in the deep zone or nowhere.
     They are now the left end of the bar strip at the foot of the frame,
     where the ramp is at full weight: the fact of the convening set small
     and dark under the name set enormous and light, a diagonal across the
     harbour with the rule running between them. */
  .fh__bar {
    grid-row: 2;
    margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 3.2vw, 2.9rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(1.25rem, 2.6vw, 2.4rem);
  }
  .fh__meta { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; }
  .fh__brow, .fh__place { margin: 0; max-width: none; }

  /* Local motion primitive: the masthead sits above motion.js's reveal
     rootMargin, so a scroll trigger can never fire here. Load-timed instead,
     same easings, no observer. */
  .fh__rule {
    display: block; height: 1px; width: 100%;
    background: var(--mark);
    transform: scaleX(0); transform-origin: left;
    animation: fhDraw 1150ms var(--ease-io) 460ms forwards;
  }
  .fh__brow, .fh__place, .fh__cue {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: fhIn var(--dur-2) var(--ease-out) forwards;
  }
  .fh__brow  { animation-delay: 260ms; }
  .fh__place { animation-delay: 380ms; }
  .fh__cue   { animation-delay: 760ms; }
  @keyframes fhIn   { from { opacity: 0; transform: translate3d(0, 12px, 0); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
  @keyframes fhDraw { to { transform: scaleX(1); } }

  /* The shared .cue primitive from base.css. It used to be a <span>, i.e. a
     word that says Scroll and cannot be followed; it is a link now, like the
     other two on the site. */

  /* ── 2. The claim ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* The pause at the foot of this beat is bounded on both sides — the claim
     above it, the plate below it — which is the only way held space reads as
     confidence instead of as a hole. */
  .fsay { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 4.4vw, 6.5rem) var(--pause); }
  /* THE MEASURED ROW. The page's width was carrying nothing: 14 of 29 marks
     began at the same x and a 460px column on the right was empty for the
     whole scroll. The beat numerals are the one element here whose job is
     positional, so they now hang on the RIGHT edge of the measure and the
     hairline runs the full width between the rail and them. The rule stops
     being a decorative underline and starts being a span with a mark at each
     end — which is the only thing that makes 1338px read as a measure. */
  /* The wipe belongs to the RULE, not to the row. It used to sit on .frow,
     which meant (a) the numeral at the far end of a 1337px measure was still
     being uncovered a second and a half after the rule started, arriving as a
     vertical slice of a glyph rather than as a numeral, and (b) the row kept
     `clip-path: inset(0)` at rest, which permanently shaved the numeral's
     optical overhang off. Territory wipes; the mark arrives. */
  /* THE GAP WAS A GUTTER, NOT A BREATH. The row inherited the page's
     column gutter — 52px at 1440 — so the hairline stopped 52px short of the
     numeral it is supposed to be measured by. On a 1267px span that reads as
     a rule that failed to arrive, not as a measure with a mark at each end.
     The gutter is a layout interval; this is an optical one, and it is set
     by eye at ~14px: enough that the 1px rule does not touch the numeral's
     stem, close enough that the two are one object. */
  .frow {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: end;
    column-gap: clamp(0.55rem, 1vw, 0.92rem);
  }
  .frow__rule {
    display: block; height: 1px; width: 100%;
    background: var(--rule);
    margin-bottom: 0.34em;
  }
  /* The house .index primitive — the site's one brass tabular numeral. This
     page used to set its own at 26.6px, which was a second index construction
     invented for one page and the only place the ladder forked. The rail the
     numerals anchor is positional, not typographic: it is the RIGHT edge of
     the measure, and the hairline spanning to it is what makes the numeral
     read as a mark on a measure rather than as a size. */
  .fnum {
    margin-right: -0.18em;
    margin-bottom: 0.1em;
  }
  .fsay__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: clamp(0.9rem, 1.6vw, 1.5rem);
    padding-top: clamp(0.9rem, 1.5vw, 1.35rem);
  }
  /* head + bridge are ONE object — the kicker — so the beat is two marks in
     a column, not three scattered ones. The interval that matters is the one
     under them, before the claim. */
  /* .fsay__who is gone: the bridge line under this eyebrow read "Founders,
     investors, elected officials." — the same fact, in the same words, as the
     ledger's "The room" row 900px below. Said once, in the beat whose job it
     is. */
  /* The wrapper carries the reveal so the two italic verbs can hang off its
     .is-in; the h2 itself carries the settle. Two elements, two transforms,
     no fight. */
  .fsay__claim { --reveal-y: 0px; }
  /* THE HINGE. "build" and "decide" are the only two words in the sentence
     that are not roman, and they are the whole claim: they arrive 210ms after
     the line they sit in, so the page's best sentence has an event of its own
     rather than one more staggered fade. */
  /* Bound to --settle, not to .is-in: the hinge is now scroll-linked and
     REVERSIBLE, so a reader who scrubs back over the claim can find the
     two-holes-in-the-sentence moment again instead of it living once for
     500ms. Each verb has its own threshold on the same ramp, so they still
     arrive behind the roman and behind each other. */
  .fsay__p :global(em) {
    display: inline-block;
    --hinge: clamp(0, calc((var(--settle, 1) - 0.42) / 0.34), 1);
    /* floored, not zeroed: at rest the verbs are present but unresolved, so
       a still of this page is a whole sentence at two brightnesses rather
       than a sentence with two words missing. The event is the resolve. */
    opacity: calc(0.26 + 0.74 * var(--hinge));
    transform: translate3d(0, calc((1 - var(--hinge)) * 0.16em), 0);
  }
  .fsay__p :global(em:last-of-type) {
    --hinge: clamp(0, calc((var(--settle, 1) - 0.60) / 0.34), 1);
  }

  .fsay__p {
    max-width: 17ch;
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 3.6rem);
    /* the settle grows from the baseline the reader is already on */
    --settle-origin: 0% 100%;
    --settle-scale: 0.945;
    --settle-shift: 4vh;
  }

  /* ── 3. The ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* The page's one change of axis. .on-panel retunes --rule / --fg-meta for
     the lighter ground; do not paint the background by hand. */
  .fbrief {
    padding-block: clamp(2.6rem, 1.8rem + 3.4vw, 5rem) clamp(3rem, 2rem + 4vw, 6rem);
  }
  /* ONE GRID, four rows: the eyebrow is the first term and the lede is the
     first definition, so the ledger's axis is established by the beat's own
     heading rather than arriving underneath it. */
  /* ONE AXIS AND ONE RIGHT EDGE. The lead row and the three rows below used
     to sit on different grids — the lead's terms hung at x=369, the rows'
     at x=305, a 143px offset repeated nowhere else on the site, which reads
     as drift rather than as a decision. Both now share this one grid, and
     the grid is the full measure, so the row hairlines end where the §02
     rule and its numeral end instead of stopping 394px short of them. */
  .fdef {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.31fr) minmax(0, 0.69fr);
    column-gap: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .fdef__t {
    text-align: right;
    margin: 0;
    /* micro caps sit above the x-height of the sentence beside them; drop
       them onto its first baseline rather than onto its box */
    transform: translateY(0.12em);
  }
  .fbrief__top { padding-top: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.7rem); }

  /* THE RULES ARE EARNED OR THEY ARE PULLED BACK. They used to span the full
     1338px measure while every sentence in the list ended by x≈968 — 419px of
     hairline with nothing under it, four times. The list now measures itself:
     the block is capped so the rules end where the sentences do. The full-
     width span belongs to .frow, whose rule has a mark at its far end. */
  /* AND HERE IS WHERE THE CAP ACTUALLY GOES. The paragraph above described
     a block that measures itself; no block did. .fdef__d carried a 62ch cap
     and the GRID did not, so every row box still ran the full 1337px measure
     while the sentence inside it stopped at 1075 — 313px of hairline with
     nothing under it, four times over. The cap is on the grid now, and the
     column split is retuned (0.24→0.31) so the definition column lands on
     exactly the measure the sentences were already setting: 397.5→1075.4.
     Not one line reflows; the rules simply stop where the reading stops.
     The lead row shares the cap so the ledger keeps ONE axis and ONE right
     edge — which is the thing the split was for. The full-width span still
     belongs to .frow, whose rule has a mark at its far end. */
  /* AND THE CAP MOVES AGAIN WHEN THE COPY DOES. 64rem was measured against
     three two-line sentences; the rows are one short line each now (the
     brief caps a section at three LINES, not three entries), so the longest
     sentence ends at 229px and a 64rem rule ran 445px past it — the same
     unearned hairline the cap was introduced to remove, at a new width. The
     cap is 32rem, which is also the plate's width one beat below: the
     ledger's right edge and the photograph's right edge are the same x. */
  .fbrief__top,
  .fdefs { max-width: 32rem; }
  /* Was clamp(1.9rem,3.6vw,3.2rem): the interval was measured under a
     two-line lede that no longer exists, and with the eyebrow alone above it
     the beat opened with a hole. */
  .fdefs { margin: clamp(1.1rem, 2.1vw, 1.7rem) 0 0; }
  .fdef--row {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-block: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.6rem);
  }
  .fdef--row:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  /* THE READING SIZE. There was none anywhere on this page. */
  .fdef__d {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-body);
    line-height: 1.58;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ── 4. The plate ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* A BOUNDED PLATE, NOT A BAND. Full-bleed made this beat the masthead
     again: same furniture, longer lens. Hung in the shell at 30rem square it
     is a different kind of object on the page — the only picture on the site
     with an edge on all four sides — and the crop it can then afford is 2.5x
     tighter than the masthead's (the masthead shows 2800 source px across
     1440 CSS, 1.94 px per CSS px; this shows 405 across 512, 0.79) while
     still rendering below 1:1 on a phone and at 1.26x on desktop. The pause
     is held by the navy around it, not by a screen edge.

     NO PARALLAX, NO SCRIM, AND THE FILE IS CUT FOR THE OVERSCAN. A bounded
     frame cannot afford the first two. At parallax 5 the transom — the only
     structure in the crop — was pushed clean off the top edge; and the placed
     scrim's top band, which exists to protect type under the fixed bar on a
     FULL-BLEED frame, landed here directly on the transom. On a hung plate
     there is no bar to protect against, so scrim="none" and the credit sits
     on the water in its own dim register.

     Figure keeps a 4% media overscan even at parallax 0 (`over` has a floor),
     so ~15 source rows are clipped off the top of any figure it draws. The
     crop is therefore cut at y 832 rather than 845: the fifteen rows the
     overscan eats are the last of the breakwater, and what the frame actually
     shows begins at the waterline. The approach below is this beat's whole
     gesture. */
  .fplate { padding-block: clamp(3.4rem, 2rem + 5.4vw, 7.5rem); }
  .fplate__frame {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    width: min(100%, 32rem);
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  }
  /* THE APPROACH. Scroll-linked, reversible: the plate sits 5.5% large while
     it is arriving and closes to 1.000 at reading position, so the duration
     of the pause is something the reader is doing rather than watching. */
  .fplate__in {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    --settle-scale: 1.055;
    --settle-shift: 0vh;
    --settle-origin: 50% 50%;
  }
  /* The plate carries NO type. Its one mark is the photograph's own credit —
     Figure's corner voice, in the deep zone of a bottom-left ramp, which is
     the only place on a photograph the meta register is legal. */

  /* ── 5. The close, on cream ─────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* THE PAGE TURNED OVER. Full-bleed .on-cream, arriving directly off the
     dark plate: L* 15 → L* 92, the largest step the system owns, spent once,
     on the page's actual job. .on-cream retunes the ink levels, the rules and
     --brass (to --brass-deep) for the light ground — never paint it by hand.
     Ground budget: hero photograph, plate photograph, this. Three, the
     ceiling, which is why the ledger's .on-panel band came off — a 13-point
     step on a 255 scale was costing a ground event and reading as nothing. */
  .frole {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: clamp(3.2rem, 2rem + 5vw, 7rem) clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 1.8vw, 3rem);
  }
  .frole__shell { position: relative; z-index: 0; }

  /* THE CUT, ANIMATED. This edge — L* 15 off the plate to L* 92 — is the
     largest tonal event on the site, and it was the only one the page did
     not move: the hero parallaxes, the claim settles, the plate approaches,
     and then the page's biggest moment simply appeared.

     A navy curtain sits over the cream and retracts to the left as the
     reader scrolls into the section, so the ground itself turns over rather
     than arriving already turned. It is scroll-linked, therefore reversible,
     and it is the same left-to-right vector the page's rules already wipe
     along — a fourth motion KIND would be decoration; this is the third,
     used at the one place that deserves it. */
  .frole__cut {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -1px;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: var(--ink-900);
    --cut: clamp(0, calc((var(--settle, 1) - 0.06) / 0.34), 1);
    transform: scaleX(calc(1 - var(--cut)));
    transform-origin: 100% 50%;
  }
  /* THE ONE BEAT THAT DOES NOT BEGIN AT THE RAIL. Above it the page opens
     flush left five times; here the eyebrow holds a narrow rail and the
     sentence starts at ~34% and runs into the right-hand column the rest of
     the page left empty for 3151px. */
  .frole__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.34fr) minmax(0, 0.66fr);
    column-gap: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 3.25rem);
    padding-top: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.9rem);
  }
  .frole__brow { margin: 0; transform: translateY(0.55em); }
  .frole__body { display: grid; justify-items: start; }
  /* THE SERIES-BREAKER, used once. --step-feature exists to lift one thing
     out of a set; on this page the set is four beats at head/poster/banner
     and this is the fifth, on the opposite ground, at the rung none of them
     use. It is allowed a wide measure because the width is the point. */
  .frole__p {
    max-width: 20ch;
    color: var(--fg);
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0 0 clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.7rem);
  }
  .frole__note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    max-width: 36ch;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The one ask on the page. The footer's quieter Forum door is suppressed on
     this route (see below), so this is the close, singular. */
  .frole__cta {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(0.7rem, 1.1vw, 1rem);
    justify-items: start;
    padding-block: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
    margin-top: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .frole__ctaText {
    color: var(--fg);
    display: inline-flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.6ch;
    position: relative;
    max-width: none;
    text-transform: none;
  }
  .frole__ctaText :global(.arrow) { width: 0.72em; align-self: center; transition: transform 520ms var(--ease-out); }
  .frole__ctaRule {
    display: block; height: 1px; width: clamp(34px, 5vw, 72px);
    background: var(--brass);
    transition: width 520ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  .frole__cta:hover .frole__ctaRule,
  .frole__cta:focus-visible .frole__ctaRule { width: clamp(54px, 9vw, 132px); }
  .frole__cta:hover :global(.arrow),
  .frole__cta:focus-visible :global(.arrow) { transform: translateX(4px); }
  .frole__ctaText::after {
    content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: -0.24em; height: 1px;
    background: currentColor; transform: scaleX(0); transform-origin: right;
    transition: transform 520ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  .frole__cta:hover .frole__ctaText::after,
  .frole__cta:focus-visible .frole__ctaText::after { transform: scaleX(1); transform-origin: left; }


  /* ── Mobile ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    /* No open half to set into at 390: the lock comes back to the rail, the
       name turns over, and the bar stacks its meta above the rule. */
    .fh__lock { width: auto; justify-self: stretch; max-width: 100%; }
    .fh__bar { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto; row-gap: 1.1rem; }
    .fh__meta { grid-column: 1 / -1; }

    /* THE LADDER HAD NO TOP RUNG ON THE FORMAT MOST READERS SEE. The
       masthead and the §2 claim are both display--poster, which on desktop
       is 86px against an 18.7px ledger — a 4.6x drop, and the composition
       (name in the water at the right, claim at the rail) tells the two
       apart anyway. On a phone --step-poster bottoms out at 36.8px, the
       claim's own size, and the two beats have neither a size difference
       nor a compositional one: the page's title arrived at 2.2x its body
       copy, 146px wide inside an 844px frame.

       --step-banner is the rung this page's own ladder note declares at the
       top and nothing on the page was spending. It cannot go on the desktop
       masthead — 126px sets "The Lion" 481px wide, which walks the name back
       across the buoy and the lattice tower the right-hand crop exists to
       stay clear of — but at 390 the name is at the rail over open water
       with nothing to collide with, so the rung is spent exactly where the
       ladder had collapsed. 36.8 → 46.4px; the claim stays at 36.8. */
    .fh__h { font-size: var(--step-banner); }

    /* One place-name, one line. The credit's 26ch cap broke "Block Island,
       Rhode Island" after "Rhode" — a state name split across a line break
       in the register whose entire job is to be unmistakably a place. It is
       one line here; it fits the measure with 128px to spare. */
    :global(.fh__bg .fig__credit) { max-width: none; white-space: nowrap; }

    .fsay { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 3.5rem) clamp(2.75rem, 10vw, 4.5rem); }
    .fsay__p { max-width: 13ch; }

    .fbrief { padding-block: clamp(2.2rem, 8vw, 3.2rem) clamp(2.6rem, 9vw, 3.8rem); }
    /* THE SECOND AXIS SURVIVES THE PHONE. The ledger used to stack flush left
       below 860, which left the whole mobile page as one column at x=24 for
       2934px. The terms now HANG in the left margin at micro size instead —
       a 4.6rem rail, right-aligned against a narrow sentence measure. That is
       the same decision the desktop makes, taken at a size a phone can pay
       for. The heading row still stacks: a lede does not belong in 26ch. */
    .fdef { grid-template-columns: 4.6rem minmax(0, 1fr); column-gap: 0.9rem; }
    .fdef__d { max-width: none; }
    .fdef__t { text-align: right; transform: translateY(0.14em); }
    .fbrief__top { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); row-gap: 0.7rem; }
    .fbrief__top .fdef__t { text-align: left; transform: none; }
    .fbrief__top, .fdefs { max-width: 100%; }

    /* At 390 the shell measure is 342px, so the square renders at 0.71x of
       its 405 native pixels — below 1:1 even at DPR2's 684. One file serves
       both formats; the portrait @1200 sibling is gone with the band. */
    .fplate { padding-block: clamp(2.6rem, 9vw, 3.8rem); }

    .frole { padding-block: clamp(2.8rem, 9vw, 4rem) clamp(2.2rem, 7vw, 3.2rem); }
    /* THE EXCEPTION SURVIVES THE PHONE. This used to stack flush at x=24,
       which meant the page's one compositional break did not exist on the
       format most readers will see — 390 reverted to a single armature for
       its whole length. A 34% rail is 110px and stops being a decision, so
       the close borrows the ledger's own mobile move instead: the eyebrow
       HANGS right-aligned in a 4.6rem rail and the sentence starts inside
       it. Same axis as §02, taken at a size a phone can pay for, and the
       close is still the only beat below the breakpoint that is indented. */
    .frole__grid { grid-template-columns: 5.8rem minmax(0, 1fr); column-gap: 0.9rem; }
    .frole__brow { text-align: right; transform: translateY(0.5em); }
    .frole__p { max-width: 16ch; }
    .frole__note { max-width: 30ch; }
    .frole__cta { margin-top: clamp(1.9rem, 7vw, 2.6rem); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .fh__h { max-width: 7ch; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .fh__rule { animation: none; transform: scaleX(1); }
    .fh__brow, .fh__place, .fh__cue { animation: none; opacity: 1; }
    .fsay__p :global(em) { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
    .fsay__p :global(em), .fsay__p :global(em:last-of-type) { --hinge: 1; }
    .frole__cut { display: none; }
    /* The ask lights, it does not travel: the rule fills at its rest length
